{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>All the words of Joseph.</b> Rashi's explanation: this is what is meant by ״וירא את העגלות אשר שלח יוסף״ — and it does not say אשר שלח פרעה. That is to say, for this reason it does not say here אשר שלח פרעה until the time of their entry into Egypt, as it is written: אשר שלח פרעה." "<b>That Joseph sent.</b> Jacob said: my son would not put his sons through departures for nothing, to trouble to bring several wagons, if my son were not [alive]. Here Rashi explains: he gave them a sign in the topic he was occupied with when he parted from him — the case of the decapitated calf (עגלה ערופה). And if you ask: what kind of sign is this? — but his father had escorted him, as we translate [in Targum Onkelos]: וישלחהו מעמק חברון ואלויה — and the escorting said to him, etc. Joseph said to his father: turn back. He said to him: my son, there is a great escorting [to come] — the passage of the decapitated calf that is destined to be added in the Torah, as it is written: ״ידנו לא שפכה את הדם״ — and would it enter your mind that a Sanhedrin spills blood? Rather: we did not send him off without provisions and without escort. And the sign within it is something that only his father and he knew. And one may say that among those wagons he sent, there were calves (עגלות) to draw them, and in this was the sign — the term falls upon the term, as we learned: עגלה המושכת בקרון פסולה לעגלה ערופה — a cow that draws a wagon is disqualified for the decapitated calf. And there is proof in the matter, as it is written: אשר שלח על פי פרעה — and did Joseph, who was the ruler, need to take permission to send wagons to his father? Rather, it was the wagons (עגלות = calves) that he needed permission for, since no cow or sow leaves there unless its mother is taken, in order that it not give birth — and these calves Joseph sent complete (שלמות), by authorization of Pharaoh." "<b>And the spirit of Jacob revived.</b> His heart returned to wellness. Rashi explains: the Shekhinah rested upon him — for it had departed from him, as stated in Tanḥuma: twenty-two years that Joseph spent in Egypt apart from his father, the holy spirit was withdrawn from Jacob and his sons — and he [Joseph] had not been far from them but a journey of four or five days, and they did not know that he was in Egypt."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 45, :notes {0 "The first word is a tag cross-referencing Rashi's comments on v.27, not a lemma from the verse.", 1 "The interpolation 'and the escorting said to him, etc.' covers an elliptical passage in the source; the flow from the Targum quotation to the dialogue is compressed and possibly missing words. The final clause about the mother being taken to prevent further births reflects ancient Near Eastern livestock control practices."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:08:51.991347184Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}